        "title": "Offer, acceptance and consideration",
        "reading": "Contract formation requires objective manifestations of assent, a sufficiently definite bargain, consideration or a legally recognized substitute, and defenses may prevent enforcement. Under common-law mirror-image analysis an acceptance changing material terms can be a counteroffer; sales of goods may trigger Uniform Commercial Code rules.\\n\\nDETAILED METHOD — First classify the transaction: goods under UCC Article 2 versus services or land under common law. Next identify objective manifestations of offer, acceptance and consideration. A reply introducing a condition may be a counteroffer under traditional common law, but UCC 2-207 requires separate analysis of a definite and seasonable expression of acceptance, express conditionality, merchant status and terms supplied by performance. WORKED EXAMPLE: a seller quotes 100 tables at $40 each; a buyer responds “accepted if shipping is free.” Discuss whether the wording is expressly conditional, whether goods rules apply, and what happens if the seller ships without expressly agreeing. COUNTERARGUMENT: the buyer might be requesting a concession rather than imposing a condition; evaluate the precise exchange and conduct. WRITING TASK: produce 300 words addressing formation, alternative outcomes, governing law and three unanswered facts.",
