        "title": "Jurisdiction and pleadings",
        "reading": "A federal court needs subject-matter jurisdiction, personal jurisdiction where required, and proper venue. Federal-question and diversity jurisdiction have distinct tests. Pleadings must satisfy applicable rules, including plausible factual allegations under federal Rule 8 and related doctrine.\\n\\nDETAILED METHOD — Analyze federal civil litigation in a fixed order: subject-matter jurisdiction, personal jurisdiction, venue, notice and service, pleadings, discovery, dispositive motions, trial and appeal. Under 28 USC 1332, diversity generally requires complete diversity and an amount in controversy exceeding $75,000, exclusive of interest and costs, subject to statutory exceptions and specialized rules. WORKED EXAMPLE: a Texas plaintiff files a $25,000 state-law contract claim against an Oklahoma company in federal court. Diversity of citizenship alone does not ordinarily satisfy the amount; consider whether any qualifying federal claim independently supplies federal-question jurisdiction. COUNTERARGUMENT: the pleaded amount might not accurately reflect the sum legally in controversy; inspect relief sought. WRITING TASK: draft a procedural roadmap and explain why a substantively valid state claim may not belong in federal court.",
