        "reading": "Claim preclusion can prevent relitigation of claims resolved by a valid final judgment, while issue preclusion can prevent relitigation of particular issues actually litigated and necessarily decided. Governing jurisdiction and exceptions matter.\n\nADVANCED REASONING LAB — At discovery, distinguish relevant proportional requests, privilege, protected work product and sanctions. Summary judgment is not a substitute for assessing witness credibility. Analyze an argument favoring each side, identify at least two additional facts that would change the conclusion, and draft a short IRAC response that clearly separates legal rule, factual application, counterargument and qualified conclusion. SOURCE CHECK: consult the governing jurisdiction's current statute or controlling opinion before treating an illustrative doctrine as current law.\n\nTOPIC-SPECIFIC WORKED OPINION LAB — PRECLUSION WORKED ANALYSIS: A plaintiff loses a final judgment concerning a contract and later sues on another theory arising from the same transaction. Claim preclusion may bar splitting a claim that could have been litigated; issue preclusion addresses a specific issue actually litigated and necessarily decided. Identify parties, finality and legal standards before applying either. A changed cause of action label does not necessarily evade claim preclusion; a distinct later event may lead to a different result.",
