        "reading": "Separate actus reus, required mental state, attendant circumstances and result elements. Analyze statutory definitions rather than assuming everyday words control. Defenses may negate an element or provide justification or excuse.\\n\\nDETAILED METHOD — Begin with the exact criminal statute and isolate each material act and mental-state element. Separate actus reus, mens rea, causation where applicable, prosecution's burden and possible defenses. WORKED EXAMPLE: a warehouse employee takes a laptop claiming it was assigned to them. An earlier text discusses selling it. If the statute requires knowing appropriation of another's property with intent to deprive, examine assignment records, contemporaneous beliefs and circumstantial proof rather than presuming guilt. COUNTERARGUMENT: a mistake of fact can negate the required mens rea, but the prosecution can use the earlier text to contest the belief. DISTINCTION: an affirmative defense may have a different allocation of proof than negation of an element. WRITING TASK: build a two-column evidence chart for prosecution and defense and finish with a qualified beyond-reasonable-doubt analysis.",
