        "reading": "Constitutional review asks whether the government actor has authority and whether an action violates a constitutional limit. Distinguish federal enumerated powers, state police powers, preemption and justiciability; the result is often tied to the requested remedy.\\n\\nDETAILED METHOD — Constitutional analysis asks who acted, which constitutional provision applies, whether the plaintiff has standing, whether the dispute is reviewable, what standard governs, and what remedy may follow. Enumerated federal powers differ from broad state police powers. WORKED EXAMPLE: Congress regulates a local activity under the Commerce Clause. Identify whether the rule addresses channels, instrumentalities, or activities substantially affecting interstate commerce; examine the activity and statutory nexus. Then ask whether a concrete injury supports the challenger's Article III standing. COUNTERARGUMENT: local economic conduct may be part of an interstate market even when one person's activity seems small; purely noneconomic conduct can be different. WRITING TASK: write separate standing, congressional-authority and remedy sections, carefully distinguishing precedents rather than reciting case names.",
        "problem": "Congress regulates commercial conduct within one state. Evaluate the text of the Commerce Clause, controlling precedent and possible federalism objections. Advanced assignment: complete the detailed method and written task in the core doctrine before checking your answer.",
        "question": "What should be tested before invoking federal supremacy?",
        "options": [
          "Whether the law is popular",
          "Whether a governor approves",
          "Constitutional validity and the scope of preemption"
        ],
        "answer": 2,
        "why": "Only valid federal law has supremacy in the relevant domain."
