        "title": "Speech restrictions and forum analysis",
        "reading": "First Amendment analysis begins with government action, the nature of the speech, the forum and the regulation. Viewpoint discrimination is especially disfavored; content-neutral time, place and manner requirements depend on applicable doctrine.\n\nADVANCED REASONING LAB — Begin with the actor, source of government power and reviewability; not every disfavored policy is unconstitutional or justiciable. Analyze an argument favoring each side, identify at least two additional facts that would change the conclusion, and draft a short IRAC response that clearly separates legal rule, factual application, counterargument and qualified conclusion. SOURCE CHECK: consult the governing jurisdiction's current statute or controlling opinion before treating an illustrative doctrine as current law.\n\nCONSTITUTIONAL LAW TEACHING CHAPTER — Begin by identifying the government actor, the specific constitutional protection or source of power, standing and the requested remedy. Do not apply identical scrutiny to free speech, equal protection, federal commerce power and procedural due process. WORKED APPLICATION: A municipality allows commercial signs for 30 days but religious-event signs for three days. Analyze whether the law classifies signs by communicative content, the relevant forum, the justification and the government's asserted interests. Compare a genuinely content-neutral size rule and explain why outcomes could differ. MODEL REASONING: a content-based distinction can trigger heightened review, but the precise rule and current controlling precedent must be confirmed.",
