        "title": "Equal protection and scrutiny",
        "reading": "Equal protection analysis identifies government action, the classification, the relevant standard of review and the fit between governmental ends and chosen means. The level of review depends on doctrine and facts.\n\nADVANCED REASONING LAB — A speech rule's content, forum and government role determine the standard; a public forum is not automatically identical to government property generally. Analyze an argument favoring each side, identify at least two additional facts that would change the conclusion, and draft a short IRAC response that clearly separates legal rule, factual application, counterargument and qualified conclusion. SOURCE CHECK: consult the governing jurisdiction's current statute or controlling opinion before treating an illustrative doctrine as current law.\n\nTOPIC-SPECIFIC WORKED OPINION LAB — EQUAL PROTECTION WORKED ANALYSIS: A city adopts a facially neutral licensing rule that disproportionately burdens one group. First identify state action and the classification, then determine whether discriminatory purpose is required under the claim. Compare a facial race classification with neutral rules having disparate effects; different review standards may apply. Analyze legislative history, administration and government justification rather than assuming effects alone prove discriminatory intent.",
