        "title": "Procedural due process and government benefits",
        "reading": "Procedural due process requires a protected life, liberty or property interest and constitutionally sufficient procedures. The timing and form of notice or hearing depend on the affected interests, error risk and government's interest.\n\nADVANCED REASONING LAB — Equal protection requires a classification and the appropriate standard of review; discriminatory impact alone does not necessarily prove discriminatory purpose. Analyze an argument favoring each side, identify at least two additional facts that would change the conclusion, and draft a short IRAC response that clearly separates legal rule, factual application, counterargument and qualified conclusion. SOURCE CHECK: consult the governing jurisdiction's current statute or controlling opinion before treating an illustrative doctrine as current law.\n\nSUBJECT-SPECIFIC DOCTRINAL CHAPTER — PROCEDURAL DUE PROCESS — Identify government deprivation of a protected life, liberty or property interest before balancing procedures. Entitlement created by a valid legal rule differs from a unilateral hope of benefits. Mathews v. Eldridge considers the private interest, error risk and value of additional safeguards, and governmental cost. WORKED FACTS: A state abruptly cancels a licensed vendor's permit after an automated flag, without a chance to correct identity errors. Determine the protected interest, urgent governmental needs, predeprivation versus postdeprivation process, and available notice and hearing. Contrast a purely discretionary new-license application with an established entitlement.",
