        "title": "Integrated case: Public forum and expressive permits",
        "reading": "First Amendment forum doctrine; assess the real reason for denial and distinguish viewpoint discrimination from neutral time-place-manner regulation. Public forum doctrine and viewpoint neutrality are distinct questions. Government can regulate the time, place and manner of speech under applicable standards, but disputed viewpoint motivation warrants examination of comparable events, policy language and contemporaneous records.\n\nLEGAL REASONING PRACTICUM: Identify the operative legal elements, apply at least two specific facts to each disputed element, analyze the strongest opposing interpretation, and explain which controlling source and missing evidence could change the outcome. Historical cases must be checked for later treatment before being relied upon.\n\nINTEGRATED CASE TEACHING CHAPTER — CASE ANALYSIS — A speech-permit dispute requires classification of the forum, whether restrictions regulate content or viewpoint, whether permit criteria leave excessive discretion, and the nature of the proposed time, place and manner requirements. WORKED OUTCOME: A city can ordinarily administer some content-neutral logistics for busy public spaces, but may not condition access on approving a speaker's viewpoint. Compare objectively defined safety rules with a permit officer's open-ended power to reject messages deemed offensive. Evaluate application procedures, alternatives, and possible emergency injunctive relief.",
