        "title": "Estates and future interests",
        "reading": "Property estates define the duration of possession and conditions on transfer. Fee simple absolute, life estate and future interests have distinct consequences. Conveyancing formalities and state law govern legal effect.\\n\\nDETAILED METHOD — Classify present and future property interests independently, remembering that land-transfer wording, jurisdiction-specific statutes and modern reforms matter. Fee simple absolute generally endures indefinitely. A life estate ends when the measuring life ends. A remainder follows natural expiration of a prior possessory estate; a reversion remains with the transferor. WORKED EXAMPLE: O conveys Blackacre “to A for life, then to B.” A has a life estate, B a remainder; explain how possession transfers when A dies. Compare “to A so long as the land is used as a park,” which may create a defeasible estate with different future interests depending on exact language. COUNTERARGUMENT: construction statutes may favor fee simple and reject forfeiture absent sufficiently clear language. WRITING TASK: diagram three conveyances, identify each interest holder and state what additional facts are needed.",
