        "title": "Integrated case: Competing deeds and notice",
        "reading": "Recording-act priority requires careful local-law and fact analysis; signing later does not alone settle title. Race-notice rules generally require a later purchaser for value to take without legally sufficient notice and to record first. Evaluate constructive and inquiry notice, apparent occupancy, timing and the state's precise recording statute before predicting the title winner.\n\nLEGAL REASONING PRACTICUM: Identify the operative legal elements, apply at least two specific facts to each disputed element, analyze the strongest opposing interpretation, and explain which controlling source and missing evidence could change the outcome. Historical cases must be checked for later treatment before being relied upon.\n\nINTEGRATED CASE TEACHING CHAPTER — CASE ANALYSIS — Resolve competing conveyances by identifying effective deed delivery, sequence of transfers, recording dates, notice and whether a later purchaser gave value in good faith. Under a notice statute a qualifying subsequent purchaser without notice may prevail even if first to record is a different person; under a race-notice statute recording order also matters. WORKED OUTCOME: Visible occupation can generate inquiry notice, defeating a purchaser's protected status if the governing standard requires inquiry. Examine exact state statutory wording, possession facts, recording chain and exceptions before choosing a winner.",
