        "title": "Acquiring possession: capture and wild animals",
        "reading": "Property law includes rules about original acquisition and possession, distinct from estates and interests in real land. Pierson v. Post is a historical New York decision examining competing claims to a fox: the majority rejected mere pursuit as insufficient to establish a possessory property right, while the dissent emphasized policy reasons for recognizing diligent pursuit. Modern possession and wildlife law may differ, and this case does not determine deed or landlord-tenant disputes.\n\nLEGAL REASONING PRACTICUM: Identify the operative legal elements, apply at least two specific facts to each disputed element, analyze the strongest opposing interpretation, and explain which controlling source and missing evidence could change the outcome. Historical cases must be checked for later treatment before being relied upon.\n\nDETAILED DOCTRINAL CHAPTER — POSSESSION AND CAPTURE — Property in wild animals traditionally required actual possession or a narrowly defined equivalent, not mere pursuit; Pierson v. Post illustrates the rule. Distinguish possession of an unowned resource from trespass and competing rights of landowners. WORKED PROBLEM: A hunter pursues an animal across a field, a second person captures it, and the first alleges ownership by pursuit. Identify what counts as capture under the historical case, then explain how modern wildlife statutes, licensing and conservation rules may supersede that default.",
