window.LAW_LEVEL2[0].lessons.push({"id":"L2-1-18","title":"Consequential damages: Hadley and proof","reading":"Contract remedies are compensatory, not automatic. Distinguish direct loss, consequential loss, reliance expenditure, restitution, and liquidated-damages clauses. For expectation damages analyze causation, foreseeability when the agreement was formed, reasonable certainty, and reasonable efforts to avoid loss. For UCC goods transactions separately check sections 2-712, 2-713 and 2-715, including incidental versus consequential damages. PRIMARY CASE: Hadley v. Baxendale (1854), 9 Exch. 341, addresses recovery of unusual mill-shutdown losses when special circumstances were not adequately communicated. It is a historical English decision, not a substitute for current governing state law. WORKED CASE: A courier delivers a factory's replacement spindle late. The factory alleges $80,000 of idle-line losses and $20,000 in lost future orders; the courier says it was told only that delivery was urgent. Identify the formation-time communications, alternative equipment, records proving lost orders, reasonable mitigation opportunities and any liability cap. Calculate no award without adequate evidence. EXAM METHOD: organize each damages category into legal rule, evidence for the claimant, evidence for defendant, uncertainty, and qualified result.","problem":"Write a 900–1,200-word two-sided legal memorandum analyzing the spindle scenario, distinguishing expectation, incidental and consequential losses. State when Hadley is persuasive rather than controlling, verify state law, examine a variation in which the courier was told the plant would shut down, and provide a source verification log. Written response requires substantive reviewer grading.","question":"Why does a special lost-profit claim require analysis of the parties' knowledge at formation?","options":["Because foreseeability of consequential loss can depend on knowledge at contract formation","Because every delayed delivery requires punitive damages","Because losses after delivery are always irrecoverable"],"answer":0,"why":"Foreseeability, certainty and mitigation constrain consequential recovery."});
window.LAW_LEVEL2[1].lessons.push({"id":"L2-2-18","title":"Relational duty and pure economic loss","reading":"Negligence requires a legally recognized duty, breach, causation and legally cognizable damages. Foreseeability alone does not necessarily establish a duty to every affected party. Pure economic losses without physical damage may be restricted under state doctrines, with exceptions that depend on contracts, professional relationships, negligent misrepresentation and other circumstances. PRIMARY CASE: Palsgraf v. Long Island Railroad Co., 248 N.Y. 339 (1928); read the majority and dissent on duty and foreseeability while recognizing it does not itself decide the pure-economic-loss rule in every jurisdiction. WORKED CASE: A warehouse employee disconnects a shared power supply; an adjacent retailer loses two days of sales but suffers no physical damage. The retailer claims foreseeable harm and reliance on a shared service; the warehouse challenges duty, causation and recovery of pure economic loss. Identify whether contractual allocation, an undertaking, a special relationship or a statute changes the outcome. Compare a variation in which refrigerated merchandise is physically ruined. RESEARCH: find controlling state supreme-court doctrine and distinguish economic loss rules for defective products from general negligence claims.","problem":"Draft a 900–1,200-word state-law memorandum advocating both sides. Explain Palsgraf's disputed duty concepts, identify relevant economic-loss limitations and recognized exceptions, analyze a physical-damage variation, and document controlling primary sources and their later treatment. Human evaluation required.","question":"What must be determined before predicting pure economic loss recovery?","options":["The controlling jurisdiction's duty and economic-loss limitations","The retailer's preferred lawyer","Whether the retailer has a logo"],"answer":0,"why":"A claim's viability depends on a recognized duty and jurisdiction-specific loss rules."});
window.LAW_LEVEL2[2].lessons.push({"id":"L2-3-18","title":"Erie and the Rules Enabling Act","reading":"Federal civil litigation requires separating subject-matter jurisdiction from choice of governing law. Diversity jurisdiction under 28 U.S.C. §1332 ordinarily requires complete diversity and an amount in controversy exceeding $75,000; corporate citizenship can include both incorporation and principal place of business. In a diversity case, distinguish federal procedural rules from state substantive rights under Erie and the Rules Enabling Act. PRIMARY CASES: Erie Railroad Co. v. Tompkins, 304 U.S. 64 (1938), rejects general federal common law; Hanna v. Plumer, 380 U.S. 460 (1965), considers a federal procedural rule and state practice. Neither supports the blanket claim that every state procedure governs federal diversity litigation. WORKED CASE: A Texas corporation sues a Louisiana corporation for $92,000. A state prefiling certificate rule differs from a potentially applicable Federal Rule. First investigate each corporation's principal place of business, then whether a federal rule actually answers the same question, its validity, and what substantive law controls. Analyze federal pleading, service and statute of limitations separately. METHOD: write a jurisdiction checklist followed by a rule-conflict flowchart and competing arguments.","problem":"Prepare a 900–1,200-word federal-court memorandum showing the jurisdiction analysis, Erie/Hanna framework, both parties' strongest arguments, and at least three uncertain facts. Record verified text and later treatment of the federal cases and governing statutory/rule provisions. Written grading is not automated.","question":"Which step precedes applying Erie in a diversity case?","options":["Determining that federal subject-matter jurisdiction exists","Choosing the easier procedural rule","Assuming all state court rules apply"],"answer":0,"why":"Jurisdiction comes first; Erie addresses the law applied by a court with jurisdiction."});
window.LAW_LEVEL2[3].lessons.push({"id":"L2-4-18","title":"Searches of smartphones after arrest","reading":"Fourth Amendment analysis should separate state action, an individual's protected interest, a government search or seizure, warrant requirements, recognized exceptions and remedies. A lawful arrest can permit certain searches incident to arrest but does not automatically permit a full digital forensic search. PRIMARY CASES: Katz v. United States, 389 U.S. 347 (1967), addresses privacy of conversations; Riley v. California, 573 U.S. 373 (2014), generally requires a warrant to search digital information on a phone seized incident to arrest absent another recognized exception. Do not conflate authority to seize a device with authority to inspect all its data. WORKED CASE: After a lawful traffic arrest, police seize a locked phone and open stored messages without consent or a new warrant. Prosecution invokes incident-to-arrest safety and evidence preservation; defense invokes Riley. Consider actual exigent circumstances, valid consent, search-warrant scope, separate authority to search, and good-faith questions where relevant. A constitutional violation does not mechanically resolve every suppression remedy. METHOD: map each government act to its legal basis and examine exceptions independently.","problem":"Produce a 900–1,200-word suppression analysis and prosecution response. Compare Riley and Katz and research any controlling circuit or state variations in remedies. Explain a fact change involving a specific and imminent danger, and label what evidence remains unavailable. Human substantive review required.","question":"What is the general rule under Riley for inspecting digital contents of a seized cell phone?","options":["A warrant is generally needed unless an exception applies","A lawful arrest allows unlimited phone searches","A cell phone contains no protected information"],"answer":0,"why":"Riley recognizes heightened privacy interests in digital contents."});
window.LAW_LEVEL2[4].lessons.push({"id":"L2-5-19","title":"Equal protection: sex classifications and neutral policies","reading":"First identify governmental conduct, the classification, and the asserted constitutional injury. Express sex classifications ordinarily receive intermediate scrutiny, requiring an exceedingly persuasive justification, while a facially neutral law with unequal statistical effects generally requires additional proof of discriminatory purpose for an Equal Protection claim. PRIMARY CASES: United States v. Virginia, 518 U.S. 515 (1996), analyzes sex-based exclusion from a publicly supported institution; Washington v. Davis, 426 U.S. 229 (1976), discusses discriminatory impact and purpose. Context and subsequent cases matter. WORKED CASE: A state reserves technical-training grants for men because it claims employment in the field has been overwhelmingly male. A woman excluded from the program challenges the classification. Evaluate asserted important objectives, the relation between sex classification and objective, stereotypes, evidence and alternative means. Compare a sex-neutral eligibility test with disparate impact but no direct proof of discriminatory intent. Separate statutory discrimination claims from constitutional claims. RESEARCH: verify both opinions, later controlling decisions and any relevant statutory schemes.","problem":"Write a 900–1,200-word constitutional memorandum addressing the grant restriction and neutral-test variation. Apply both Supreme Court cases, set out the strongest state and applicant positions, identify unknown evidence of purpose and fit, and verify subsequent treatment. Human substantive assessment required.","question":"Which standard usually governs an express sex classification by a state?","options":["Intermediate scrutiny","No review of government choices","Automatic criminal liability"],"answer":0,"why":"Sex-based classifications ordinarily trigger heightened intermediate scrutiny."});
window.LAW_LEVEL2[5].lessons.push({"id":"L2-6-19","title":"Adverse possession and tacking","reading":"Adverse possession law varies substantially across states. Common concepts are actual, open and notorious, exclusive, hostile or claim-of-right, and continuous possession for a statutory period, sometimes supplemented by tax payment or color-of-title requirements. Hostility usually concerns inconsistent possession, not personal dislike. Tacking may require privity; seasonal use can be consistent with the property's nature under some state laws. PRIMARY CASE: Howard v. Kunto, 3 Wash. App. 393, 477 P.2d 210 (1970), discusses seasonal occupancy and tacking in Washington. Do not apply that jurisdiction's rule nationally. WORKED CASE: One owner fences and gardens a ten-foot strip, transfers the property after eight years, and the purchaser uses the strip for another five. The neighbor has a deed and objects after a survey. Create a dated timeline, identify possession interruptions and signs visible to the record owner, and analyze the significance of the deed description and transfer. Determine the governing state's statutory duration before reaching a conclusion; an easement claim is analytically distinct. METHOD: use an element-by-element proof matrix, then evaluate counterarguments and remedies.","problem":"Draft a 900–1,200-word memorandum for record owner and possessor, including an evidentiary timeline, competing tacking arguments, statutory analysis in the chosen jurisdiction and citations checked for current status. Explain why Howard may be persuasive but not controlling. Written evaluation required.","question":"What is essential before deciding whether thirteen years of possession suffices?","options":["The governing state's requirements and limitations period","The neighbor's personality","The fence color"],"answer":0,"why":"Elements and statutory periods differ by jurisdiction."});
window.LAW_LEVEL2[6].lessons.push({"id":"L2-7-18","title":"Authority checking and negative treatment","reading":"Strong legal writing starts with primary law, precise quotations, jurisdictional hierarchy and verified treatment of cited authorities. Separate the holding from dicta, procedural history and advocates' characterizations. A Supreme Court opinion may bind lower federal and state courts on federal issues, but state-law questions depend on applicable state authority and federal court jurisdictional context. Citators may identify reversal, overruling, criticism or distinguishing; a negative flag is a research lead, not a full legal conclusion. PRIMARY CASE LAB: Compare Erie Railroad Co. v. Tompkins, 304 U.S. 64 (1938), with Hanna v. Plumer, 380 U.S. 460 (1965). A student draft says: 'Erie requires all state procedures in federal diversity cases.' Evaluate whether this is an overstatement by examining both holdings and the specific federal procedural issue. WORKFLOW: state research question, map binding jurisdictions, retrieve full opinions from authoritative sources, check case subsequent history, use precise pin cites, verify statute/rule currency, document the search date, and rewrite the proposition with appropriately narrow language. Preserve uncertainty where treatment could not be checked.","problem":"WRITTEN RESEARCH EXAM — 900–1,200 words. Deliver a corrective CREAC memorandum on the Erie assertion and an authority log for at least four sources identifying court, date, relevant holding, precise pinpoint, later treatment, verification date and research gaps. Draft both supporting and opposing analysis. No multiple-choice question can substitute for human review of source accuracy.","question":"Which practice best validates a legal proposition?","options":["Reading the relevant holding and checking subsequent treatment","Relying only on a search summary","Citing whichever case has an appealing name"],"answer":0,"why":"Citation accuracy requires full-text verification, jurisdictional analysis and checking later treatment."});

// Independent issue-spotting guidance and assessment criteria for supplemental examinations.
{const exam=window.LAW_LEVEL2[0].lessons.find(l=>l.id==='L2-1-18');exam.problem+='\n\nEXAMINER CHECKLIST L2-1-18 — Classify direct versus consequential loss and apply foreseeability at formation; evaluate loss certainty, proof, mitigation, and a limitation clause.\n\nSUBSTANTIVE RUBRIC (100 POINTS) — Governing law and damages classification 20; fact-specific foreseeability and causation 25; certainty and mitigation 20; counterarguments and changed facts 20; primary source verification and organization 15.\n\nGRADING LIMITATION — The quiz does not grade the written examination. A qualified independent reviewer must assess both sides, verify current primary law and substantively evaluate reasoning before assigning a score.';}
{const exam=window.LAW_LEVEL2[1].lessons.find(l=>l.id==='L2-2-18');exam.problem+='\n\nEXAMINER CHECKLIST L2-2-18 — Identify a recognized duty and the claimed protected interest; apply jurisdiction-specific economic-loss limitations; analyze exceptions and a physically damaged inventory variation.\n\nSUBSTANTIVE RUBRIC (100 POINTS) — Duty and elements 20; jurisdiction-specific economic-loss treatment 25; factual causation and damages 20; counterargument and changed facts 20; authority verification and writing 15.\n\nGRADING LIMITATION — The quiz does not grade the written examination. A qualified independent reviewer must assess both sides, verify current primary law and substantively evaluate reasoning before assigning a score.';}
{const exam=window.LAW_LEVEL2[2].lessons.find(l=>l.id==='L2-3-18');exam.problem+='\n\nEXAMINER CHECKLIST L2-3-18 — Analyze federal subject-matter jurisdiction before Erie; identify any real collision between state law and a Federal Rule; apply Erie, Hanna, and Rules Enabling Act rather than treating all state practices alike.\n\nSUBSTANTIVE RUBRIC (100 POINTS) — Jurisdiction 20; Erie/Hanna framework 25; specific rule-conflict reasoning 20; opposing position and changed facts 20; authority verification and writing 15.\n\nGRADING LIMITATION — The quiz does not grade the written examination. A qualified independent reviewer must assess both sides, verify current primary law and substantively evaluate reasoning before assigning a score.';}
{const exam=window.LAW_LEVEL2[3].lessons.find(l=>l.id==='L2-4-18');exam.problem+='\n\nEXAMINER CHECKLIST L2-4-18 — Separate constitutional search, warrant and exception analysis from suppression; distinguish physical phone seizure from digital examination; analyze actual emergency evidence and consent.\n\nSUBSTANTIVE RUBRIC (100 POINTS) — Protected interest and search 20; Riley and exceptions 25; fact-specific prosecution and defense arguments 20; suppression and altered facts 20; authority verification and writing 15.\n\nGRADING LIMITATION — The quiz does not grade the written examination. A qualified independent reviewer must assess both sides, verify current primary law and substantively evaluate reasoning before assigning a score.';}
{const exam=window.LAW_LEVEL2[4].lessons.find(l=>l.id==='L2-5-19');exam.problem+='\n\nEXAMINER CHECKLIST L2-5-19 — Identify governmental sex classification and intermediate scrutiny; analyze objective and fit; distinguish neutral rule with disparate impact from proof of discriminatory purpose.\n\nSUBSTANTIVE RUBRIC (100 POINTS) — Classification and standard 20; objective and fit 25; neutral rule and purpose 20; opposing arguments and changed facts 20; authority verification and writing 15.\n\nGRADING LIMITATION — The quiz does not grade the written examination. A qualified independent reviewer must assess both sides, verify current primary law and substantively evaluate reasoning before assigning a score.';}
{const exam=window.LAW_LEVEL2[5].lessons.find(l=>l.id==='L2-6-19');exam.problem+='\n\nEXAMINER CHECKLIST L2-6-19 — Construct possession timeline; apply state-specific limitations and elements; examine tacking, privity and interruption; distinguish adverse possession from easement.\n\nSUBSTANTIVE RUBRIC (100 POINTS) — Statute and timelines 20; possession elements 25; tacking/privity evidence 20; competing claims and changed facts 20; authority verification and writing 15.\n\nGRADING LIMITATION — The quiz does not grade the written examination. A qualified independent reviewer must assess both sides, verify current primary law and substantively evaluate reasoning before assigning a score.';}
{const exam=window.LAW_LEVEL2[6].lessons.find(l=>l.id==='L2-7-18');exam.problem+='\n\nEXAMINER CHECKLIST L2-7-18 — Extract actual holdings from Erie and Hanna; distinguish federal procedural rules and state substantive law; document exact quotations, hierarchy, later treatment and research date.\n\nSUBSTANTIVE RUBRIC (100 POINTS) — Authority mapping 20; holding and proposition accuracy 25; rule synthesis and CREAC 20; counterargument and limits 20; source log, pin cites and clarity 15.\n\nGRADING LIMITATION — The quiz does not grade the written examination. A qualified independent reviewer must assess both sides, verify current primary law and substantively evaluate reasoning before assigning a score.';}

// Seven capstone examination packets: evaluated writing is not automatically graded.
{const cap=window.LAW_LEVEL2[0].lessons.find(l=>l.id==='L2-1-17');if(!cap)throw Error('Missing L2-1-17');cap.reading+="\n\nCAPSTONE FINAL EXAM L2-1-17 — INTEGRATED FACT PATTERN: A manufacturer and distributor exchange conflicting forms for 500 custom refrigeration units. The distributor pays a deposit; the manufacturer ships 300 units late, claims a raw-material shortage, and announces it may not deliver the balance. The distributor covers at a higher price, claims lost supermarket contracts, and demands return of the deposit. The seller alleges a liability cap and buyer failure to provide promised specifications.";cap.problem+="\n\nFINAL EXAM DELIVERABLES — Analyze UCC applicability and §2-207 forms, performance and excuse, adequate assurances and anticipatory repudiation, cover and consequential damages, foreseeability, mitigation and limitation clauses. Draft contract timeline and damages schedule, distinguish amounts supported from hypothetical amounts. Submit a 1,500–2,000-word IRAC/CREAC memorandum, a one-page issue and evidence chart, two opposing arguments, and a 250-word reflection explaining unresolved facts. Identify jurisdiction and authority hierarchy. For at least four primary authorities record citation, relevant holding or statutory language, source location, checked date, and any later adverse treatment; do not claim verification that has not occurred.\n\nCAPSTONE RUBRIC — Issue spotting and doctrine 25 points; legally grounded fact analysis 30 points; competing positions and changed-fact analysis 15 points; quality and currency of primary authorities 20 points; organization and professional writing 10 points. A human substantive reviewer must assess the complete written response; quiz correctness alone cannot award capstone mastery.";}
{const cap=window.LAW_LEVEL2[1].lessons.find(l=>l.id==='L2-2-17');if(!cap)throw Error('Missing L2-2-17');cap.reading+="\n\nCAPSTONE FINAL EXAM L2-2-17 — INTEGRATED FACT PATTERN: An apartment complex ignores complaints about a deteriorating stairwell. A contractor working on the stairs leaves materials blocking an exit. During a power outage a tenant falls and is injured; a visiting delivery worker witnesses the fall and reports emotional harm. The landlord and contractor blame each other.";cap.problem+="\n\nFINAL EXAM DELIVERABLES — Analyze distinct duties owed to tenant, visitor and worker, landowner/contractor negligence, factual and proximate causation, comparative fault and contribution, premises duties and emotionally distressed bystanders under a specified state\\'s law. Prepare a claim-defendant matrix and evidence plan. Submit a 1,500–2,000-word IRAC/CREAC memorandum, a one-page issue and evidence chart, two opposing arguments, and a 250-word reflection explaining unresolved facts. Identify jurisdiction and authority hierarchy. For at least four primary authorities record citation, relevant holding or statutory language, source location, checked date, and any later adverse treatment; do not claim verification that has not occurred.\n\nCAPSTONE RUBRIC — Issue spotting and doctrine 25 points; legally grounded fact analysis 30 points; competing positions and changed-fact analysis 15 points; quality and currency of primary authorities 20 points; organization and professional writing 10 points. A human substantive reviewer must assess the complete written response; quiz correctness alone cannot award capstone mastery.";}
{const cap=window.LAW_LEVEL2[2].lessons.find(l=>l.id==='L2-3-17');if(!cap)throw Error('Missing L2-3-17');cap.reading+="\n\nCAPSTONE FINAL EXAM L2-3-17 — INTEGRATED FACT PATTERN: A resident sues an out-of-state retailer and an affiliated shipping corporation over a defective product bought online. The complaint names the wrong corporate entity after limitations expires; defendants remove the case and seek dismissal for jurisdiction and pleading deficiencies. A disputed email is withheld as purported work product.";cap.problem+="\n\nFINAL EXAM DELIVERABLES — Analyze §1332 citizenship and amount, removal timing and forum-defendant issues where relevant, personal jurisdiction, venue, Rule 12(b)(6), Rule 15(c) relation back, discovery privilege and work product, and summary judgment sequencing. Produce procedural timeline with independent threshold questions. Submit a 1,500–2,000-word IRAC/CREAC memorandum, a one-page issue and evidence chart, two opposing arguments, and a 250-word reflection explaining unresolved facts. Identify jurisdiction and authority hierarchy. For at least four primary authorities record citation, relevant holding or statutory language, source location, checked date, and any later adverse treatment; do not claim verification that has not occurred.\n\nCAPSTONE RUBRIC — Issue spotting and doctrine 25 points; legally grounded fact analysis 30 points; competing positions and changed-fact analysis 15 points; quality and currency of primary authorities 20 points; organization and professional writing 10 points. A human substantive reviewer must assess the complete written response; quiz correctness alone cannot award capstone mastery.";}
{const cap=window.LAW_LEVEL2[3].lessons.find(l=>l.id==='L2-4-17');if(!cap)throw Error('Missing L2-4-17');cap.reading+="\n\nCAPSTONE FINAL EXAM L2-4-17 — INTEGRATED FACT PATTERN: Two participants discuss a warehouse burglary. One buys tools; the other claims they withdrew before the offense. Police arrest one suspect at a traffic stop, examine a seized smartphone without a warrant, and obtain a disputed confession. Prosecutors allege conspiracy and accomplice liability.";cap.problem+="\n\nFINAL EXAM DELIVERABLES — Analyze statutory elements and agreement, overt acts where required, mens rea, withdrawal, accomplice liability, relevant constitutional search and interrogation safeguards, suppression and burden of proof. Distinguish allegations from proof beyond a reasonable doubt. Submit a 1,500–2,000-word IRAC/CREAC memorandum, a one-page issue and evidence chart, two opposing arguments, and a 250-word reflection explaining unresolved facts. Identify jurisdiction and authority hierarchy. For at least four primary authorities record citation, relevant holding or statutory language, source location, checked date, and any later adverse treatment; do not claim verification that has not occurred.\n\nCAPSTONE RUBRIC — Issue spotting and doctrine 25 points; legally grounded fact analysis 30 points; competing positions and changed-fact analysis 15 points; quality and currency of primary authorities 20 points; organization and professional writing 10 points. A human substantive reviewer must assess the complete written response; quiz correctness alone cannot award capstone mastery.";}
{const cap=window.LAW_LEVEL2[4].lessons.find(l=>l.id==='L2-5-18');if(!cap)throw Error('Missing L2-5-18');cap.reading+="\n\nCAPSTONE FINAL EXAM L2-5-18 — INTEGRATED FACT PATTERN: A city leases its public auditorium to a privately managed nonprofit, subsidizes its operations, and participates in scheduling. The nonprofit rejects a political speaking group while allowing comparable speakers. A statewide funding program separately creates an express sex-based preference for youth training.";cap.problem+="\n\nFINAL EXAM DELIVERABLES — Analyze state action for the auditorium decision, forum and speech principles if governmental action is established, content and viewpoint distinctions, standing and remedies, then separately analyze the sex classification under Equal Protection. Do not conflate constitutional and possible statutory theories. Submit a 1,500–2,000-word IRAC/CREAC memorandum, a one-page issue and evidence chart, two opposing arguments, and a 250-word reflection explaining unresolved facts. Identify jurisdiction and authority hierarchy. For at least four primary authorities record citation, relevant holding or statutory language, source location, checked date, and any later adverse treatment; do not claim verification that has not occurred.\n\nCAPSTONE RUBRIC — Issue spotting and doctrine 25 points; legally grounded fact analysis 30 points; competing positions and changed-fact analysis 15 points; quality and currency of primary authorities 20 points; organization and professional writing 10 points. A human substantive reviewer must assess the complete written response; quiz correctness alone cannot award capstone mastery.";}
{const cap=window.LAW_LEVEL2[5].lessons.find(l=>l.id==='L2-6-18');if(!cap)throw Error('Missing L2-6-18');cap.reading+="\n\nCAPSTONE FINAL EXAM L2-6-18 — INTEGRATED FACT PATTERN: A purchaser discovers that a neighbor has used a fenced strip for decades, a utility has an unrecorded claimed easement, and a tenant says the landlord concealed serious water intrusion. A developer now proposes construction that may increase noise and drainage onto neighboring parcels.";cap.problem+="\n\nFINAL EXAM DELIVERABLES — Analyze recorded title and inquiry notice, adverse possession period and tacking under chosen state law, easement creation/scope, tenant habitability and remedies, nuisance and potential equitable relief. Create parcel sketch in words, title chain, timeline and remedy matrix. Submit a 1,500–2,000-word IRAC/CREAC memorandum, a one-page issue and evidence chart, two opposing arguments, and a 250-word reflection explaining unresolved facts. Identify jurisdiction and authority hierarchy. For at least four primary authorities record citation, relevant holding or statutory language, source location, checked date, and any later adverse treatment; do not claim verification that has not occurred.\n\nCAPSTONE RUBRIC — Issue spotting and doctrine 25 points; legally grounded fact analysis 30 points; competing positions and changed-fact analysis 15 points; quality and currency of primary authorities 20 points; organization and professional writing 10 points. A human substantive reviewer must assess the complete written response; quiz correctness alone cannot award capstone mastery.";}
{const cap=window.LAW_LEVEL2[6].lessons.find(l=>l.id==='L2-7-17');if(!cap)throw Error('Missing L2-7-17');cap.reading+="\n\nCAPSTONE FINAL EXAM L2-7-17 — INTEGRATED FACT PATTERN: An attorney asks for a memorandum arguing that a federal diversity court must follow every state procedural practice. A search result quotes Erie without context, overlooks Hanna, cites an unpublished state case as binding and contains a quotation that may be inaccurate.";cap.problem+="\n\nFINAL EXAM DELIVERABLES — Locate primary sources; identify issue, jurisdiction, hierarchy, accurate holding, procedural posture, subsequent treatment and quotation accuracy. Correct the proposition using Erie and Hanna, compare potentially relevant controlling circuit authority, create a research log, draft balanced CREAC and prepare a five-minute oral defense. Submit a 1,500–2,000-word IRAC/CREAC memorandum, a one-page issue and evidence chart, two opposing arguments, and a 250-word reflection explaining unresolved facts. Identify jurisdiction and authority hierarchy. For at least four primary authorities record citation, relevant holding or statutory language, source location, checked date, and any later adverse treatment; do not claim verification that has not occurred.\n\nCAPSTONE RUBRIC — Issue spotting and doctrine 25 points; legally grounded fact analysis 30 points; competing positions and changed-fact analysis 15 points; quality and currency of primary authorities 20 points; organization and professional writing 10 points. A human substantive reviewer must assess the complete written response; quiz correctness alone cannot award capstone mastery.";}

// Doctrinal accuracy refinements from primary-source review.
{const lesson=window.LAW_LEVEL2.flatMap(c=>c.lessons).find(l=>l.id==='L2-1-10');if(!lesson)throw Error('Missing L2-1-10');lesson.reading+='\n\nPRECISION CHECK L2-1-10 — '+"PRIMARY-LAW PRECISION CHECK — New York UCC § 2-609 requires reasonable grounds for insecurity and a written demand; suspension pending adequate assurance must be commercially reasonable. A justified demand left inadequately answered within a reasonable period not exceeding 30 days constitutes repudiation. Thirty days is an outer limit, not an automatic waiting period. Between merchants, commercial standards govern reasonableness. Distinguish a bare concern from a justified demand and clear anticipatory repudiation. Official source: https://www.nysenate.gov/legislation/laws/UCC/2-609. State-specific adoption and later cases still require verification.";}
{const lesson=window.LAW_LEVEL2.flatMap(c=>c.lessons).find(l=>l.id==='L2-5-19');if(!lesson)throw Error('Missing L2-5-19');lesson.reading+='\n\nPRECISION CHECK L2-5-19 — '+"PRIMARY-OPINION PRECISION CHECK — United States v. Virginia, 518 U.S. 515, 533 (1996), requires an exceedingly persuasive justification for governmental sex classifications: important governmental objectives and means substantially related to those objectives; a post-hoc or stereotyped explanation cannot substitute for genuine justification. Distinguish government equal-protection claims from private statutory claims and facially neutral policies where discriminatory purpose may need to be shown. Supreme Court source: https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/boundvolumes/518bv.pdf. Later binding treatment remains to be checked.";}

// Focused legal precision checks for remaining Level 2 subjects.
{const lesson=window.LAW_LEVEL2.flatMap(c=>c.lessons).find(l=>l.id==='L2-2-18');if(!lesson)throw Error('Missing L2-2-18');lesson.reading+="\n\nPRECISION REVIEW L2-2-18 — Palsgraf duty distinction: Cardozo's majority addresses duty owed to a foreseeable plaintiff, while the Andrews dissent considers broader duty and proximate cause. Neither opinion establishes a national pure-economic-loss rule; identify the controlling state's doctrine independently. Case: https://www.courtlistener.com/?q=Palsgraf%20Long%20Island%20Railroad.";}
{const lesson=window.LAW_LEVEL2.flatMap(c=>c.lessons).find(l=>l.id==='L2-3-18');if(!lesson)throw Error('Missing L2-3-18');lesson.reading+="\n\nPRECISION REVIEW L2-3-18 — Erie/Hanna framework: First establish federal subject-matter jurisdiction. If a Federal Rule directly answers the disputed procedural question, evaluate validity and applicability under the Rules Enabling Act and Hanna. If not, work through Erie and subsequent choice-of-law doctrine; neither case says every state procedural rule applies in federal diversity court. Primary cases: Erie Railroad Co. v. Tompkins, 304 U.S. 64 (1938); Hanna v. Plumer, 380 U.S. 460 (1965).";}
{const lesson=window.LAW_LEVEL2.flatMap(c=>c.lessons).find(l=>l.id==='L2-4-18');if(!lesson)throw Error('Missing L2-4-18');lesson.reading+="\n\nPRECISION REVIEW L2-4-18 — Riley v. California, 573 U.S. 373 (2014), generally prohibits searching digital contents of a phone incident to arrest without a warrant. This is different from physically securing the phone; case-specific exigent circumstances may justify a separate exception. A claimed exception must be tied to actual facts; suppression and its exceptions are separate questions. Opinion: https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/13-132.";}
{const lesson=window.LAW_LEVEL2.flatMap(c=>c.lessons).find(l=>l.id==='L2-6-19');if(!lesson)throw Error('Missing L2-6-19');lesson.reading+="\n\nPRECISION REVIEW L2-6-19 — Howard v. Kunto, 3 Wash. App. 393, 477 P.2d 210 (1970), evaluates seasonal occupancy and tacking of successive possessors in a Washington boundary dispute. The court does not establish an across-the-country statutory period or declare all seasonal use continuous. Ascertain the selected state's current statute, privity requirements, notice and interruptions before reaching any result.";}
{const lesson=window.LAW_LEVEL2.flatMap(c=>c.lessons).find(l=>l.id==='L2-7-18');if(!lesson)throw Error('Missing L2-7-18');lesson.reading+="\n\nPRECISION REVIEW L2-7-18 — Case verification means reading the relevant passage of an actual opinion and recording jurisdiction, procedural posture, holding and later treatment. Erie does not mean that all state court procedures govern in diversity litigation; Hanna separately addresses potentially controlling valid Federal Rules. Do not claim a citator check or quotation verification unless performed and dated. Distinguish binding versus persuasive opinions and clearly mark unresolved treatment.";}

const L2_KEY='law-academy-level2-v1';
function renderLevel2(){const courses=window.LAW_LEVEL2;let record={};try{record=JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem(L2_KEY)||'{}')||{}}catch(e){}const esc=s=>String(s).replace(/&/g,'&amp;').replace(/</g,'&lt;').replace(/>/g,'&gt;').replace(/"/g,'&quot;');const save=()=>{try{localStorage.setItem(L2_KEY,JSON.stringify(record));return true}catch(e){return false}};
const home=()=>{shell('<div class="pagehead"><span class="gold-label">LEVEL 2 · FIRST-YEAR LAW</span><h1>1L Core Curriculum</h1><p>Seven foundational subjects. Initial authored modules are available; the full 1L syllabus remains in development.</p></div><div class="level-list">'+courses.map((c,i)=>'<button class="level-row" data-l2course="'+i+'"><span class="level-icon">'+(i+1)+'</span><span class="level-text"><strong>'+esc(c.title)+'</strong><small>'+c.lessons.filter(l=>record[l.id]?.correct).length+' / '+c.lessons.length+' lesson checks passed</small></span><span class="level-arrow">›</span></button>').join('')+'</div><button id="l2realcases" class="gold-button">Real court cases · predict the outcome</button><button id="l2exam" class="gold-button">Timed 1L writing exam</button><button id="l2back" class="button secondary">Back to Academy</button>','courses');document.querySelectorAll('[data-l2course]').forEach(b=>b.onclick=()=>showCourse(+b.dataset.l2course));document.querySelector('#l2back').onclick=renderCatalog;document.querySelector('#l2exam').onclick=()=>showExam();document.querySelector('#l2realcases').onclick=()=>showRealCases();};
const showExam=()=>{const prompts=[
['Contracts','A retail buyer orders 500 chairs at $65 each for delivery December 10. A manufacturer acknowledges the order but changes delivery to December 20 and adds arbitration. Both are merchants. Buyer accepts December 20 delivery and claims lost promotional sales. Analyze UCC formation, additional and different terms, performance, provable damages, and each side’s strongest argument.'],
['Torts','A hotel receives a loose-stair-rail report at 8 a.m. and schedules repair for 4 p.m. At noon a guest falls, claiming the rail detached. A witness says luggage caused the trip. Analyze applicable duty, notice, breach, competing causes, damages, comparative fault and evidence required.'],
['Civil Procedure','An Illinois citizen sues a Texas citizen in federal court in Texas for $90,000. Defendant disputes personal jurisdiction and venue. Plaintiff also wants to sue a Texas company on another contract. Distinguish citizenship, diversity, amount, claims, personal jurisdiction, venue and available procedural motions.'],
['Criminal Law','A worker takes a laptop from the storage room, claiming it was assigned to them. A coworker reports an earlier statement about selling it. The jurisdiction requires knowingly taking another person’s property with intent to deprive. Evaluate offense elements, mistake of fact, conflicting evidence, burdens of proof and competing conclusions.'],
['Constitutional Law','A state university allows registered student groups to book meeting rooms. It denies an invitation to a controversial speaker citing safety and staffing, although a comparably sized event expressing opposing views was allowed. Analyze forum classification, viewpoint bias, neutral rationales, disputed evidence and relief.'],
['Property','Before subdivision, the owner used a gravel driveway across the southern parcel to reach the northern parcel. The southern buyer later blocks access; another possible route needs costly construction. Analyze express, implied, necessity and prescriptive easements, scope, notice and governing state law.'],
['Legal Research & Writing','A client asks about auto-renewing subscriptions. A state consumer statute was amended after two appellate cases, contains an industry exception and may have a delayed effective date. Draft an objective memo outline, current-law research plan, history and subsequent-treatment checks, adverse authority discussion and missing-fact list.']
];const key='law-academy-level2-exam-v1';const metaKey='law-academy-level2-exam-timing-v1';const limit=140*60*1000;let timing={};try{timing=JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem(metaKey)||'{}')||{}}catch(e){};if(!Number.isFinite(timing.startedAt)){timing={startedAt:Date.now(),submittedAt:null};try{localStorage.setItem(metaKey,JSON.stringify(timing))}catch(e){}}let answers={};try{answers=JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem(key)||'{}')||{}}catch(e){};shell('<div class="pagehead"><span class="gold-label">FORMATIVE · LEVEL 2</span><h1>Timed 1L Writing Exam</h1><p>Seven original issue-spotting prompts, suggested 140 minutes total. This is ungraded practice; it is not the held-out bar examination.</p></div>'+prompts.map((p,i)=>'<div class="review-item"><h3>'+(i+1)+'. '+p[0]+'</h3><p>'+p[1]+'</p><label for="l2exam'+i+'">Issue, rule, application, counterargument, conclusion</label><textarea class="writing-field" rows="7" id="l2exam'+i+'"></textarea></div>').join('')+'<p id="l2examClock" role="timer">Suggested writing window: 140 minutes</p><button id="l2examSubmit" class="gold-button">Submit practice attempt</button><p id="l2examStatus" role="status">Drafts saved on this device as you type.</p><button id="l2examBack" class="button secondary">Back to Level 2</button>','courses');const elapsed=Math.max(0,Date.now()-timing.startedAt);const clock=document.querySelector('#l2examClock');const remaining=Math.max(0,Math.ceil((limit-elapsed)/60000));clock.textContent=timing.submittedAt?'Attempt submitted for independent review.':(remaining?remaining+' minutes remain in suggested window.':'Suggested 140-minute window elapsed. You may continue practicing.');const submit=document.querySelector('#l2examSubmit');const status=document.querySelector('#l2examStatus');const fields=()=>prompts.map((_,i)=>document.querySelector('#l2exam'+i));const lock=()=>{fields().forEach(x=>x.disabled=true);submit.disabled=true};if(timing.submittedAt)lock();submit.onclick=()=>{const written=fields().filter(x=>x.value.trim()).length;if(written!==7){status.textContent='Complete all seven responses before submission ('+written+'/7 present).';return}timing.submittedAt=Date.now();try{localStorage.setItem(metaKey,JSON.stringify(timing));lock();clock.textContent='Attempt submitted for independent review.';status.textContent='Submitted locally. No score is assigned automatically.'}catch(e){timing.submittedAt=null;status.textContent='Unable to save submission. Copy answers before leaving.'}};prompts.forEach((p,i)=>{const field=document.querySelector('#l2exam'+i);field.value=answers[i]||'';field.oninput=()=>{answers[i]=field.value;try{localStorage.setItem(key,JSON.stringify(answers))}catch(e){document.querySelector('#l2examStatus').textContent='Storage unavailable. Copy your answers before leaving.'}}});document.querySelector('#l2examBack').onclick=home};
const REAL_CASES=[{"course":"Contracts","name":"Lucy v. Zehmer","court":"Supreme Court of Virginia (1954)","source":"https://law.justia.com/cases/virginia/supreme-court/1954/4272-1.html","facts":"A buyer and sellers signed a writing for sale of a farm after drinking together. The sellers later said it was all a joke. The buyer understood a serious bargain had been struck.","question":"Should a party's undisclosed claim that they were joking automatically defeat the apparent agreement?","options":["Yes, whenever a party privately intended a joke","No; a court considers reasonable outward words and conduct","Yes, because land contracts never can be enforced"],"answer":1,"outcome":"The Virginia Supreme Court found an enforceable agreement based on objective manifestations of assent and directed specific performance. The sellers' undisclosed intent to joke did not control.","reason":"Objective contract formation focuses on reasonably communicated conduct; intoxication and equitable remedy arguments still require factual analysis.","task":"Write an IRAC paragraph distinguishing a private joke from objectively apparent assent."},{"course":"Torts","name":"Palsgraf v. Long Island Railroad Co.","court":"New York Court of Appeals (1928)","source":"https://law.justia.com/cases/new-york/court-of-appeals/1928/248-n-y-339-1928.html","facts":"Railroad workers helped a passenger board a moving train. His package fell, containing concealed fireworks. The explosion caused scales to fall some distance away and injure a bystander.","question":"Under the majority's approach, was the railroad liable to this injured passenger?","options":["No; the majority found no duty to this unforeseeable plaintiff","Yes, every negligent act creates unlimited liability","Yes, because negligence never requires duty"],"answer":0,"outcome":"The New York Court of Appeals reversed for the railroad. Chief Judge Cardozo's majority reasoned the risk to this plaintiff was not reasonably foreseeable; Judge Andrews dissented with a broader duty approach.","reason":"Separate a duty owed to the plaintiff from careless conduct directed toward someone else, and examine the dissent's approach.","task":"Compare the majority's duty analysis with the dissent's proximate-cause framework."},{"course":"Civil Procedure","name":"International Shoe Co. v. Washington","court":"U.S. Supreme Court (1945)","source":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/326/310","facts":"A Delaware shoe company had no Washington office, but employed sales representatives who continuously solicited orders there. Washington sought unpaid unemployment-fund contributions.","question":"Could Washington assert personal jurisdiction based on the company's in-state activities?","options":["No, only a corporation's incorporation state can hear such claims","Yes, systematic forum contacts connected to the claim supported jurisdiction","No, unless the company rented its headquarters in Washington"],"answer":1,"outcome":"The U.S. Supreme Court upheld Washington's exercise of jurisdiction. The company's continuous in-state activity generated obligations connected to that activity.","reason":"The minimum-contacts and fairness analysis depends on the quality, nature, and connection of contacts to the dispute; later decisions have refined the doctrine.","task":"Identify the forum contacts and explain why this decision does not authorize jurisdiction over every unrelated claim."},{"course":"Criminal Law","name":"Gideon v. Wainwright","court":"U.S. Supreme Court (1963)","source":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/372/335","facts":"Clarence Gideon, unable to afford counsel, was denied an appointed lawyer at his Florida state felony trial and represented himself. He was convicted and challenged that denial.","question":"Did the Constitution require appointed counsel for an indigent defendant in this state felony prosecution?","options":["No, appointed lawyers are required only in federal prosecutions","No, a person always must retain private counsel","Yes; the Sixth Amendment right applies to state felony prosecutions through the Fourteenth Amendment"],"answer":2,"outcome":"The U.S. Supreme Court unanimously reversed, holding that counsel must be provided for indigent defendants in state felony prosecutions and overruling Betts v. Brady.","reason":"Connect the Sixth Amendment guarantee with incorporation under the Fourteenth Amendment; later case law governs other prosecution categories.","task":"Explain the constitutional rule and the consequences of denial of appointed counsel."},{"course":"Constitutional Law","name":"Riley v. California","court":"U.S. Supreme Court (2014)","source":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/13-132","facts":"Police seized a smartphone during a lawful arrest and accessed its digital contents without first obtaining a warrant, citing the search-incident-to-arrest exception.","question":"Does a lawful arrest alone generally allow a warrantless search of the phone's digital contents?","options":["Yes, all data is automatically searchable","No; police generally need a warrant absent another exception","Yes, but only for newer phones"],"answer":1,"outcome":"The U.S. Supreme Court held that officers generally must obtain a warrant to search digital information on a seized cellphone incident to arrest, subject to other recognized exceptions.","reason":"The privacy implications of vast digital data differ from ordinary physical items; narrowly assess any actual exigency.","task":"Explain the difference between seizing a phone and searching its digital contents."}];
const showRealCases=()=>{const esc=s=>String(s).replace(/&/g,'&amp;').replace(/</g,'&lt;').replace(/>/g,'&gt;').replace(/"/g,'&quot;');shell('<div class="pagehead"><span class="gold-label">LEVEL 2 · ACTUAL COURT DECISIONS</span><h1>Decide the Real Case</h1><p>Real parties and verified historical decisions. Make your prediction before revealing the outcome. These holdings are contextual, not a substitute for current-law research.</p></div>'+REAL_CASES.map((c,i)=>'<div class="review-item"><h3>'+esc(c.course)+' · '+esc(c.name)+'</h3><p>'+esc(c.court)+'</p><p>'+esc(c.facts)+'</p><p><strong>Decision question:</strong> '+esc(c.question)+'</p>'+c.options.map((o,k)=>'<label style="display:block;margin:9px 0"><input type="radio" name="realcase'+i+'" value="'+k+'"> '+esc(o)+'</label>').join('')+'<label for="realcaseDraft'+i+'">Explain your reasoning before revealing the outcome</label><textarea id="realcaseDraft'+i+'" class="writing-field" rows="4"></textarea><button class="gold-button" data-reveal="'+i+'">Reveal what the court decided</button><div id="realcaseResult'+i+'" role="status"></div></div>').join('')+'<button id="realcaseBack" class="button secondary">Back to Level 2</button>','courses');document.querySelectorAll('[data-reveal]').forEach(b=>b.onclick=()=>{const i=+b.dataset.reveal,c=REAL_CASES[i];const choice=document.querySelector('input[name="realcase'+i+'"]:checked');const draft=document.querySelector('#realcaseDraft'+i).value.trim();const target=document.querySelector('#realcaseResult'+i);if(!choice||!draft){target.textContent='Select a prediction and explain your reasoning first.';return}target.textContent='';const p=document.createElement('p');p.textContent=(+choice.value===c.answer?'Your prediction matches the decision. ':'Your prediction differs from the decision. ')+c.outcome+' Why: '+c.reason+' Writing challenge: '+c.task;target.append(p);const link=document.createElement('a');link.href=c.source;link.target='_blank';link.rel='noopener noreferrer';link.textContent='Read the original court decision';target.append(link);b.disabled=true});document.querySelector('#realcaseBack').onclick=home};
const INLINE_CASES=[...REAL_CASES.filter(c=>c.course!=='Criminal Law'),{course:'Criminal Law',name:'Morissette v. United States',court:'U.S. Supreme Court (1952)',facts:'A defendant collected discarded bomb casings from a government range, claiming he believed the materials abandoned.',question:'Could criminal intent be disregarded merely because the federal statute was silent?',outcome:'The Supreme Court reversed, requiring proper treatment of intent for this theft-like offense.',reason:'Traditional theft-related crimes generally require a culpable mental state; assess statutory language and exceptions.',source:'https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/342/246',task:'Analyze intent, mistake of fact and how the jury should be instructed.'},{course:'Property',name:'Pierson v. Post',court:'New York Supreme Court of Judicature (1805)',facts:'One hunter pursued a fox; another intercepted and killed it, leading to a dispute over who first acquired ownership.',question:'Was pursuit without capture sufficient to establish ownership of the fox?',outcome:'The majority held mere pursuit insufficient. The dissent favored the pursuing hunter.',reason:'The case illustrates acquisition by possession of wild animals, not a rule about estates in land.',source:'https://history.nycourts.gov/case/pierson-v-post/',task:'Contrast majority and dissent and distinguish possessory acquisition from land estates.'}];const MORE_LESSON_CASES=[{"course":"Contracts","lesson":1,"name":"Hadley v. Baxendale","facts":"A mill's broken crankshaft was delayed in shipment; owners sought lost profits from the carrier.","question":"Are all consequential lost profits recoverable from a breach?","outcome":"The court limited recovery to foreseeable loss arising naturally or within parties' contemplation when contracting.","reason":"Foreseeability of special circumstances affects consequential damages.","task":"Identify what notice to the carrier could have changed the analysis.","source":"https://www.bailii.org/ew/cases/EWHC/Exch/1854/J70.html"},{"course":"Torts","lesson":2,"name":"MacPherson v. Buick Motor Co.","facts":"An automobile wheel collapsed and injured a purchaser; the manufacturer had bought the wheel from another supplier.","question":"Was a direct contract with the manufacturer essential to an ordinary negligence claim?","outcome":"The New York Court of Appeals permitted negligence liability without contractual privity for a foreseeably dangerous defective product.","reason":"A manufacturer may owe a duty of care to foreseeable users beyond direct contracting parties.","task":"Distinguish negligent product manufacture from modern strict product liability.","source":"https://law.justia.com/cases/new-york/court-of-appeals/1916/217-n-y-382-1916.html"},{"course":"Civil Procedure","lesson":2,"name":"Taylor v. Sturgell","facts":"Different individuals brought Freedom of Information Act litigation over the same documents.","question":"Does a prior judgment ordinarily bind a new litigant who was not a party?","outcome":"The Supreme Court rejected broad virtual representation and reaffirmed limited exceptions to the rule against nonparty preclusion.","reason":"Due process limits binding a person to another's judgment.","task":"Identify the recognized exceptions and distinguish claim from issue preclusion.","source":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/07-371"},{"course":"Criminal Law","lesson":2,"name":"United States v. Resendiz-Ponce","facts":"A defendant challenged an indictment for attempted illegal reentry because it did not describe the particular overt act.","question":"Must every attempted-offense indictment specify the precise overt act?","outcome":"The Supreme Court held that this indictment's use of 'attempted' and its factual allegations sufficiently informed the accused of the charge.","reason":"The word attempt can encompass the overt-act component, depending on the charging context.","task":"Contrast pleading sufficiency with the proof of intent and the substantial step.","source":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/05-998"},{"course":"Constitutional Law","lesson":1,"name":"Tinker v. Des Moines","facts":"Public-school students wore black armbands to protest the Vietnam War; school officials suspended them.","question":"Can a public school prohibit peaceful political expression without evidence of substantial disruption?","outcome":"The Supreme Court protected the students' expression where the record did not establish material and substantial interference.","reason":"Student speech has distinctive standards; do not automatically transpose them to all public forums.","task":"Apply the substantial-disruption standard and compare government forum doctrines.","source":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/393/503"},{"course":"Property","lesson":3,"name":"Van Sandt v. Royster","facts":"A purchaser discovered an underground sewer crossing her property to serve neighboring lots formerly under common ownership.","question":"Can an easement arise from prior use despite absence of an express grant?","outcome":"The Kansas Supreme Court recognized an implied easement under the circumstances of prior common use and reasonable necessity.","reason":"Prior use, apparent or discoverable character, severance and necessity matter.","task":"Compare implied easement from prior use with necessity and prescription.","source":"https://law.justia.com/cases/kansas/supreme-court/1938/33-475-0.html"}];
const ADVANCED_LESSON_CASES=[{"course":"Criminal Law","lesson":5,"name":"Nix v. Williams","facts":"After unconstitutional police questioning, evidence of a victim's body was found while a large independent search was already underway.","question":"Can evidence be admitted if a lawful search would inevitably have found it?","outcome":"The Supreme Court held that evidence concerning the body was admissible because it would inevitably have been discovered through lawful search efforts.","reason":"The government must show inevitable discovery by a preponderance of the evidence; speculation is insufficient.","task":"Compare inevitable discovery with independent source and state what search evidence matters.","source":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/467/431"},{"course":"Property","lesson":6,"name":"Kelo v. City of New London","facts":"A city used eminent domain in connection with a broader economic redevelopment plan, with property ultimately planned for private development.","question":"Does the federal Public Use Clause categorically prohibit economic-development takings involving private use?","outcome":"The Supreme Court upheld the redevelopment plan's public purpose under the federal constitution, while recognizing that states can impose more restrictive limits.","reason":"Public use and just compensation are separate requirements, and stricter state constraints may apply.","task":"Analyze the majority and dissents; research whether the governing state imposes stricter public-use rules.","source":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/04-108"}];
const NEW_LESSON_CASES=[{"course":"Civil Procedure","lesson":6,"name":"Erie Railroad Co. v. Tompkins","facts":"A Pennsylvania resident injured by a train in Pennsylvania brought a diversity case in federal court and sought to rely on federal general common law.","question":"Could the federal diversity court use a general federal common-law negligence rule instead of controlling state substantive law?","outcome":"The Supreme Court rejected federal general common law and required application of state substantive law in diversity proceedings.","reason":"The Erie doctrine distinguishes substantive state law from federal procedural governance, refined by later decisions.","task":"Explain the substantive/procedural distinction and identify the controlling law.","source":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/304/64"},{"course":"Constitutional Law","lesson":7,"name":"Washington v. Davis","facts":"Applicants challenged a police-department test that disproportionately excluded Black applicants, alleging an equal-protection violation.","question":"Is disparate impact alone ordinarily enough for a constitutional equal-protection challenge to a facially neutral test?","outcome":"The Supreme Court held that discriminatory impact alone did not establish the constitutional violation; discriminatory purpose mattered.","reason":"The constitutional inquiry differs from some statutory anti-discrimination frameworks.","task":"Separate impact evidence, proof of discriminatory purpose, and possible statutory claims.","source":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/426/229"}];
const DOCTRINE_CASES=[{"course":"Civil Procedure","lesson":7,"name":"Bell Atlantic Corp. v. Twombly","facts":"Telephone customers alleged that major companies conspired to restrain trade, relying primarily on allegations of parallel business conduct.","question":"Do conclusory assertions of agreement without plausible supporting facts ordinarily suffice?","outcome":"The Supreme Court required enough factual matter to make the asserted antitrust agreement plausible rather than merely conceivable.","reason":"Pleading plausibility concerns adequately alleged facts, not proof by a preponderance at the pleading stage.","task":"Rewrite a conclusory complaint paragraph using relevant, noninvented factual allegations.","source":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/05-1126"},{"course":"Constitutional Law","lesson":8,"name":"United States v. Lopez","facts":"A student was prosecuted under a federal statute prohibiting firearm possession in a school zone; the challenged law did not require a commerce connection in each case.","question":"Was this federal prohibition as enacted sustained under Congress's Commerce Clause authority?","outcome":"The Supreme Court held the Gun-Free School Zones Act as enacted exceeded Congress's commerce power.","reason":"The decision distinguished economic activity and emphasized limits on aggregating non-economic local conduct.","task":"Discuss enumerated powers and distinguish later commerce cases.","source":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/514/549"}];
const NEXT_CASES=[{"course":"Civil Procedure","lesson":8,"name":"Celotex Corp. v. Catrett","facts":"An asbestos plaintiff opposed summary judgment after the defendant argued she lacked adequate evidence on a necessary element of her claim.","question":"Can a summary-judgment movant point to an absence of evidence on an element the other party must prove?","outcome":"The Supreme Court held a movant need not always produce affirmative evidence negating an opponent's claim and may point to an absence of proof on an essential element.","reason":"Rule 56 burdens and the evidentiary record govern; do not mistake legal sufficiency for resolving witness credibility.","task":"Identify what evidence the nonmovant must present and what factual disputes remain.","source":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/477/317"},{"course":"Criminal Law","lesson":8,"name":"Miranda v. Arizona","facts":"Police questioned suspects in custody without the procedural warnings later required by the Court before custodial interrogation.","question":"What procedural safeguards apply to custodial questioning when prosecutors seek to use the statements?","outcome":"The Supreme Court established Miranda safeguards for custodial interrogation, subject to subsequent doctrinal limitations.","reason":"Analyze custody, interrogation, warnings, waiver and remedies separately from the Sixth Amendment right to counsel.","task":"Compare precharge interrogation and postattachment Sixth Amendment situations.","source":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/384/436"}];
const WAVE_CASES=[{"course":"Constitutional Law","lesson":10,"name":"Lujan v. Defenders of Wildlife","facts":"Environmental groups challenged a federal agency rule but could not establish concrete plans connecting members to the threatened locations.","question":"Can generalized concern without sufficiently concrete injury establish federal Article III standing?","outcome":"The Supreme Court found standing lacking because the required injury showing was insufficient.","reason":"Article III standing requires injury in fact, traceability and redressability.","task":"Analyze member injury and possible remedies without assuming a policy disagreement alone creates standing.","source":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/504/555"},{"course":"Property","lesson":10,"name":"Boomer v. Atlantic Cement Co.","facts":"Neighbors challenged dirt, smoke, and vibration produced by a cement plant, seeking an injunction and damages.","question":"Must a court automatically shut down a business whenever a private nuisance is established?","outcome":"The New York Court of Appeals permitted a permanent-damages remedy instead of an unconditional injunction.","reason":"A nuisance determination and selection of equitable remedy are separate questions.","task":"Explain the majority's remedy choice and arguments for an injunction.","source":"https://law.justia.com/cases/new-york/court-of-appeals/1970/26-n-y-2d-219-0.html"}];
const CLASSIC_CASES=[{"course":"Civil Procedure","lesson":10,"name":"Wal-Mart Stores, Inc. v. Dukes","facts":"Employees sought certification of a large nationwide class challenging discretionary employment decisions at multiple stores.","question":"Was broad class certification justified without a common answer tying together the challenged decisions?","outcome":"The Supreme Court rejected the proposed class certification, emphasizing the need for common contentions capable of classwide resolution.","reason":"Rule 23 commonality requires more than many plaintiffs presenting related grievances.","task":"Compare common questions, representative proof and individual supervisor discretion.","source":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/10-277"},{"course":"Criminal Law","lesson":10,"name":"In re Winship","facts":"A juvenile was adjudicated delinquent based on findings under a preponderance standard for acts that would be criminal if committed by an adult.","question":"Must every element of a criminal offense be established beyond reasonable doubt in this delinquency proceeding?","outcome":"The Supreme Court held that due process requires proof beyond a reasonable doubt of every fact necessary to constitute the charged criminal act.","reason":"Reasonable doubt is a constitutional safeguard against erroneous criminal adjudication.","task":"Explain why a jury instruction shifting the burden of intent is problematic.","source":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/397/358"}];
const CURRENT_CASES=[{"course":"Torts","lesson":11,"name":"New York Times Co. v. Sullivan","facts":"A public official sued over inaccuracies in an advertisement criticizing official responses to civil-rights demonstrations.","question":"Could a public official recover for a defamatory falsehood about official conduct without proving knowing or reckless falsity?","outcome":"The Supreme Court held that a public official must prove actual malice for the covered defamation claim.","reason":"Actual malice means knowledge of falsity or reckless disregard for truth, not personal hostility.","task":"Apply the constitutional fault standard to the newspaper's evidence.","source":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/376/254"},{"course":"Constitutional Law","lesson":12,"name":"Kennedy v. Bremerton School District","facts":"A public-school football coach challenged discipline after praying at midfield following games; the parties disputed the nature and impact of the expression.","question":"Did the Court continue using the Lemon and endorsement tests as its controlling Establishment Clause framework?","outcome":"The Supreme Court ruled for the coach and disapproved reliance on the Lemon and endorsement approaches, emphasizing history and tradition.","reason":"Distinguish private religious expression from government coercion or sponsored exercises; later factual contexts may differ.","task":"Compare a personal postgame prayer with an officially directed mandatory religious exercise.","source":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/21-418"}];
const COMPANION_CASES=[{"course":"Torts","lesson":12,"name":"Vosburg v. Putney","facts":"A school pupil kicked another pupil during class, aggravating an existing injury.","question":"Does battery require intending the full severity of the resulting harm?","outcome":"The court recognized liability arising from the unlawful intentional contact, despite the unexpected severity of the injury.","reason":"Distinguish intention to make contact from intention to cause a particular injury.","task":"Analyze consent, the classroom setting, and the scope of resulting liability.","source":"https://law.justia.com/cases/wisconsin/supreme-court/1891/80-wis-523-0.html"},{"course":"Constitutional Law","lesson":13,"name":"Mathews v. Eldridge","facts":"An agency terminated disability benefits following documentary review without a pretermination evidentiary hearing.","question":"Is a live evidentiary hearing always required before disability benefits are terminated?","outcome":"The Supreme Court found the procedures sufficient in that setting, applying a three-factor balancing framework.","reason":"Consider private interests, erroneous-deprivation risk and value of safeguards, and governmental burdens.","task":"Apply the three factors to a dispute involving conflicting eyewitness accounts.","source":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/424/319"}];
const PRIVACY_LESSON_CASES=[{"course":"Constitutional Law","lesson":15,"name":"Katz v. United States","facts":"Agents electronically listened to a private conversation made inside a public telephone booth without physically entering the booth.","question":"Does the Fourth Amendment apply only if government agents physically intrude into a protected property space?","outcome":"The Supreme Court held the electronic surveillance implicated Fourth Amendment protections, explaining that the Amendment protects people, not merely places.","reason":"A justified expectation of privacy can matter independently of physical trespass; later digital-search precedents refine the doctrine.","task":"Identify the search and evaluate how Katz differs from an ordinary observation in public.","source":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/389/347"}];
const INLINE_CASE_MAP={'Contracts':0,'Torts':0,'Civil Procedure':0,'Criminal Law':0,'Constitutional Law':0,'Property':0};
const showCourse=i=>{const c=courses[i];shell('<div class="pagehead"><span class="gold-label">LEVEL 2 · '+esc(c.id)+'</span><h1>'+esc(c.title)+'</h1></div><div class="level-list">'+c.lessons.map((l,j)=>'<button class="level-row" data-l2lesson="'+j+'"><span class="level-text"><strong>'+esc(l.title)+'</strong><small>'+(l.title.startsWith('Capstone:')?'Capstone · written work needs independent review':(l.problem.includes('SUBSTANTIVE RUBRIC (100 POINTS)')?'Advanced written examination · independent review required':(record[l.id]?.correct?'Lesson check passed':'Study and practice')))+'</small></span><span class="level-arrow">›</span></button>').join('')+'</div><button id="l2back" class="button secondary">All Level 2 courses</button>','courses');document.querySelectorAll('[data-l2lesson]').forEach(b=>b.onclick=()=>showLesson(i,+b.dataset.l2lesson));document.querySelector('#l2back').onclick=home};
const showLesson=(i,j)=>{const l=courses[i].lessons[j];const linked=PRIVACY_LESSON_CASES.find(c=>c.course===courses[i].title&&c.lesson===j)||COMPANION_CASES.find(c=>c.course===courses[i].title&&c.lesson===j)||CURRENT_CASES.find(c=>c.course===courses[i].title&&c.lesson===j)||CLASSIC_CASES.find(c=>c.course===courses[i].title&&c.lesson===j)||WAVE_CASES.find(c=>c.course===courses[i].title&&c.lesson===j)||NEXT_CASES.find(c=>c.course===courses[i].title&&c.lesson===j)||DOCTRINE_CASES.find(c=>c.course===courses[i].title&&c.lesson===j)||NEW_LESSON_CASES.find(c=>c.course===courses[i].title&&c.lesson===j)||ADVANCED_LESSON_CASES.find(c=>c.course===courses[i].title&&c.lesson===j)||MORE_LESSON_CASES.find(c=>c.course===courses[i].title&&c.lesson===j)||(((courses[i].title==='Constitutional Law'||courses[i].title==='Property')?j===5:j===0)?INLINE_CASES.find(c=>c.course===courses[i].title):null);const researchCase=courses[i].title==='Legal Research & Writing'&&j===0?INLINE_CASES.find(c=>c.course==='Civil Procedure'):null;shell('<div class="pagehead"><span class="gold-label">LEVEL 2 · '+esc(courses[i].title)+'</span><h1>'+esc(l.title)+'</h1></div><div class="review-item"><h3>Core doctrine</h3><p style="white-space:pre-line">'+esc(l.reading)+'</p></div>'+(researchCase?'<div class="review-item"><h3>Case research lab: '+esc(researchCase.name)+'</h3><p>Read the actual decision and prepare a case brief: procedural history, facts, issue, holding, controlling rule, reasoning, and treatment by later courts. Distinguish the historical holding from current doctrine.</p><p><a href="'+researchCase.source+'" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Read original judicial opinion</a></p></div>':'')+(linked?'<div class="review-item"><h3>Real case: '+esc(linked.name)+'</h3><p>'+esc(linked.facts)+'</p><p><strong>Predict:</strong> '+esc(linked.question)+'</p><textarea id="inlineCaseReason" class="writing-field" rows="4" placeholder="Write your reasoning before revealing"></textarea><button id="inlineCaseReveal" class="gold-button">Reveal actual court decision</button><div id="inlineCaseResult" role="status"></div></div>':'')+'<div class="review-item"><h3>Applied legal problem</h3><p style="white-space:pre-line">'+esc(l.problem)+'</p><label for="l2draft">Your written analysis (practice, not graded)</label><textarea id="l2draft" class="writing-field" rows="9"></textarea></div><div class="review-item"><h3>Knowledge check</h3>'+(l.title.startsWith("Capstone:")||l.problem.includes("SUBSTANTIVE RUBRIC (100 POINTS)")?'<p><strong>Written examination notice:</strong> The question below checks one concept only. Written responses are saved as practice drafts and require independent substantive review; no essay grade or academic certification is awarded here.</p>':'')+'<p>'+esc(l.question)+'</p>'+l.options.map((o,k)=>'<label style="display:block;margin:10px 0"><input type="radio" name="l2choice" value="'+k+'"> '+esc(o)+'</label>').join('')+'<button id="l2submit" class="gold-button">Check answer</button><p id="l2result" role="status"></p></div><button id="l2back" class="button secondary">Back to course</button>','courses');if(linked){document.querySelector('#inlineCaseReveal').onclick=()=>{const reasoning=document.querySelector('#inlineCaseReason').value.trim(),out=document.querySelector('#inlineCaseResult');if(reasoning.length<35){out.textContent='Explain your prediction in at least 35 characters before revealing the court decision.';return}out.textContent=linked.outcome+' Legal reasoning: '+linked.reason+' Writing task: '+linked.task+' ';const a=document.createElement('a');a.href=linked.source;a.textContent='Read court decision';a.rel='noopener noreferrer';a.target='_blank';out.append(a)}};const draft=document.querySelector('#l2draft');draft.value=record[l.id]?.draft||'';draft.oninput=()=>{record[l.id]={...(record[l.id]||{}),draft:draft.value};save()};document.querySelector('#l2submit').onclick=()=>{const chosen=document.querySelector('input[name="l2choice"]:checked');if(!chosen){document.querySelector('#l2result').textContent='Choose an answer first.';return}const correct=+chosen.value===l.answer;record[l.id]={...(record[l.id]||{}),correct:correct||(record[l.id]?.correct||false)};const persisted=save();document.querySelector('#l2result').textContent=(correct?'Correct. ':'Not yet. ')+l.why+(persisted?'':' Warning: progress could not be saved.');};document.querySelector('#l2back').onclick=()=>{record[l.id]={...(record[l.id]||{}),draft:draft.value};save();showCourse(i)}};home();}

